  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1209 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nA warm day is already in play across the Mid-South as 594 dam\nridding from the four corners region has begun to infiltrate into\nthe Mississippi Valley. Current temperatures have already climbed\ninto the upper 80s to lower 90s with triple digit heat indices in\nsome places. As we head into the afternoon hours, we'll continue\nto warm into the mid 90s mainly along and west of the Mississippi\nRiver. These areas are also expected to see heat indices in\nexcess of 105F warranting a Heat Advisory remaining in effect\nthrough 8PM this evening. As with the past few days, heading into\npeak daytime heating this afternoon, primarily diurnally driven\npop-up showers and thunderstorms are anticipated. Once again,\nwe're looking at a high CAPE and low shear environment, typical\nwith summertime convection. As far as values go, forecast\nsoundings are indicating upwards of 2500 J/kg of SBCAPE, around\n15 kts of effective shear, 0-3km lapse rates around 8.0 C/km, and\nPWATs around 1.8\". Something to note: we don't have as great of\nan environment nor quite as much moisture as yesterday, however,\nstronger thunderstorms could dip into enough instability to\ncreate a tall enough updraft for a damaging wind gust or two,\nwith the best coverage over north Mississippi.\n\nMoving through the week, temperatures will continue to warm with\ndewpoints in the low to mid 70s as aforementioned ridging and a\nsecondary 597dam ridge over the southeastern CONUS continue to\nbroaden and heights continue to rise. A pretty decent dewpoint\nspread exists across deterministic and ensemble members with HREF\ntrending lower, in the low 70s and the GFS/NBM trending higher in\nthe mid to upper 70s. Depending on how high dewpoints are able to\nclimb, will greatly determine how high heat indices become.\nNonetheless, Heat Advisories will be warranted across at least\nportions of the Mid-South each day as heat indices increase to\n105+F. As far as shower and thunderstorm chances go, moving into\nmid-week, as heights increase, chances decrease. Though, pop-up,\ndiurnally driven activity can't be ruled out each afternoon,\nespecially east of the Mississippi River.\n\nAs we approach the latter half of the week and into the weekend,\nguidance continues to trend warmer as ridging of the southeastern\nCONUS looks to dominate the Mid-South with little to no influence\nfrom troughing to our north. Previous runs were pushing us more\ninto a northwest flow regime with troughing digging further\nsouth, however, confidence in this solution continues to\ndecrease. There remains around a 4-5 degree spread in high\ntemperatures late week and into the weekend, but the 10th\npercentile is in the upper 90s with the 90th percentile in the\nlower 100s. Bottom line, it will be hot for the foreseeable\nfuture. If ridging holds strong rain chances will also continue\nto decrease, potentially creating a dry spell beginning Thursday\nand lasting through at least the seven day period. Even if a\nnorthwest flow regime is able to infiltrate into the Mid-South,\ngiven how strong this ridge looks to become we will be hot and\nhumid despite a potential shift in the upper pattern. Heat\nindices in excess of 110F are not out of the question.\n\nAEH",
